## Setup

This notebook is self-contained. The cell above installs AL directly, no separate node, no cookie, nothing to attach to. The first run compiles AL and its dependencies from scratch, which takes a minute or two. Reopening this notebook later is fast, since `Mix.install` caches by the exact dependency and config combination. State here is throwaway. It lives only for this notebook's session and disappears when the session ends.

If you already have a real AL node running and want the persistent, shared store instead, see the [intro](./intro.livemd) for how to attach to it instead.

`Mix.install` has to finish running before AL's `run` macro and `~AL` sigil can be brought into scope, so that happens in its own cell here.

```elixir
use AL

AL.Branch.head()
```

## Creating durable classes and instances

AL has an object system in the style of CLOS and Smalltalk, and aims to make writing live, open polymorphisms easy. The details of this will become more apparent later, but the basic ideas you need to know right now are that you can declare classes with `@name #{options}.`, give them methods with `name >> selector` clauses, and create instances of those classes with `new`. Let's try the well-known example of a light switch, which has on/off state.

You can re-evaluate the cell freely. Declaring a class or naming an instance again replaces the old declaration, and the `flick` clauses in the cell replace the method's earlier clauses rather than adding to them. The head of a clause sits between bars on its own line, and the body follows. Inside `flick` below you'll see `get`/`set_slot`, which read and write an object's own state. They're ordinary methods on `object`, not special syntax, so `get Obj key Value` is itself a message send, the same as `flick Self` is.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  @switch
  #{super => object, ivars => [#{default => "off", domain => ["on", "off"], name => state}]}.

  switch >> flick
  | Self |
  get Self state "on",
  set_slot Self state "off".

  switch >> flick
  | Self |
  get Self state "off",
  set_slot Self state "on".

  new switch #{name => switch_instance_1, state => "on"} S.
  """
end
```

In AL, durable objects are represented by atom IDs. You can inspect basic information about a durable object using `examine/2`.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  examine switch_instance_1 Info.
  """
end
```

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  examine switch Info.
  """
end
```

As you can see, `switch` is a class which inherits from `object`. It provides the method `flick`, which we defined. It was used to create `switch_instance_1`. `switch`es have a 'state' which is either on or off.

`examine` isn't the only way to see this. `class/2` and `super/2` are themselves ordinary queryable relations, not just something a class declaration sets once and hides away.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  class switch_instance_1 C.
  super switch S.
  """
end
```

Let's see what happens if we 'flick' the 'switch'.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  flick switch_instance_1.
  get switch_instance_1 state State.
  """
end
```

It should work! And if you evaluate again, you'll see the state change too. `flick switch_instance_1` is shorthand: it's exactly the same as writing the send out explicitly, `send switch_instance_1 flick`.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  flick switch_instance_1.
  get switch_instance_1 state State.
  """
end
```

AL is also capable of enforcing slot invariants.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  set_slot switch_instance_1 state sideways.
  """
end
```

## Method dispatch

A send and `label` solve different problems. Dispatch decides which method can answer a message. It does not search for or construct every possible receiver.

For a ground receiver, AL dispatches a message like this:

1. Determine the receiver's exact class. Lists, numbers, and maps have structural classes; durable objects have recorded classes; custom values carry their value class.
2. Walk that class and its superclasses in method-resolution order.
3. Select the nearest class that defines the selector. That provider owns the call; an overriding method does not silently fall through to an inherited provider when its clauses fail.
4. Try that method's clauses by ordinary unification and backtracking.
5. `call_next_method` explicitly continues with the next provider in the resolution order.

If the selected provider has no applicable clause, the send reaches `does_not_understand`. It does not silently try an inherited implementation.

For an open receiver, AL keeps the receiver symbolic:

1. Find the classes and singleton objects that directly provide the selector.
2. Create one logical branch for each possible effective provider.
3. On a class branch, post both `isa Receiver Provider` and the fact that this provider was selected for this selector.
4. Run the method clauses relationally. Their heads and bodies can narrow the receiver further.
5. Let later `class`, `isa`, slot, `dif`, and other constraints discard incompatible branches.

The provider regions are selector-relative. If `car` overrides `move`, the `vehicle` branch for `move` excludes cars, while a subclass that inherits `vehicle`'s implementation remains in the `vehicle` branch. The receiver becomes concrete only if ordinary unification determines it or the program explicitly calls `label`.

## Inheritance

As you may have already figured out, AL supports inheritance. This is not the scope to explain how inheritance itself works, but instead we will demonstrate a couple features that may be of benefit. For example, you can use `call_next_method` for method continuation and rely on inherited slots.

```elixir
run do 
  ~AL"""
  @counted_switch
  #{super => switch, ivars => [#{default => 0, name => count, type => number}]}.

  counted_switch >> flick
  | Self |
  get Self count C,
  = C1 (+ C 1),
  set_slot Self count C1,
  call_next_method Self.

  new counted_switch #{name => counted_switch_instance_1} C.
  """
end
```

```elixir
run do 
  ~AL"""
  flick counted_switch_instance_1.
  examine counted_switch_instance_1 Info.
  """
end

```

In fact, AL even supports multiple inheritance. Let's take a look at the classic diamond inheritance problem. To demonstrate this, we'll need another kind of switch. Let's take one that flips its colour every time that it is turned on. For now we have a couple of limitations: Firstly, we must label the var, since constraints aren't durable in the database. Secondly, we don't have an ordering on the domain, meaning we can't express an exact cycle.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  @colour_cycle_switch
  #{super => switch, ivars => [#{domain => ["red", "green", "blue"], name => colour}]}.

  colour_cycle_switch >> flick
  | Self |
  get Self state "off",
  get Self colour C,
  in_domain C1 ["red", "green", "blue"],
  dif C C1,
  label C1,
  set_slot Self colour C1,
  call_next_method Self.

  colour_cycle_switch >> flick
  | Self |
  get Self state "on",
  call_next_method Self.

  new colour_cycle_switch #{colour => "red", name => cc_switch_instance_1} C.
  """
end
```

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  flick cc_switch_instance_1.
  examine cc_switch_instance_1 Info.
  """
end
```

Regardless this is sufficient for demonstrating how AL manages diamond inheritance. Here's a little tip: you can search a method by its object-associated name using the following snippet. For example:

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  findall [Obj, Id] Objids (method Obj flick Id).
  """
end
```

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  @diamond_switch
  #{super => [counted_switch, colour_cycle_switch]}.

  new diamond_switch #{colour => "red", name => diamond_switch_instance_1, state => "off"} C.
  """
end
```

The order in which the two supers are topologically sorted actually doesn't matter for flick, since the operations we defined are commutative.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  flick diamond_switch_instance_1.
  examine diamond_switch_instance_1 Info.
  """
end
```

## Categories: importing methods without inheritance

`categories:` is a different way to share methods, and it isn't inheritance at all. A category is just an ordinary object with some methods on it. `import` copies those methods directly onto a class by their shared method id, with no super edge created. The class's own super chain is completely unaffected.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  new category #{ivars => [], name => glowable, super => object} Cat.

  glowable >> glow
  | Self Result |
  = Result shiny.

  @lamp
  #{super => object, categories => [glowable]}.

  new lamp #{name => lamp_1} W.
  glow lamp_1 Result.
  """
end
```

`lamp` answers `glow`, but its super is still just `object`.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  super lamp S.
  """
end
```

## Printing objects

Every object can describe itself for printing, through a relation called `print_object`. It doesn't print anything by itself. It just relates `self` to some representation of it, and something else decides what to do with that (like `listing` below). The default version, defined on `object`, just reports the object's own class.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  print_object switch_instance_1 Description.
  """
end
```

Any class can override `print_object` to describe its own instances better, the same way `printOn:` works in Smalltalk. We can add this straight to `switch` without touching its existing `flick` clauses, since `print_object` is a different selector: the clauses a cell gives replace only the methods it names.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  switch >> print_object
  | Self Description |
  get Self state S,
  = Description [switch, S].

  print_object switch_instance_1 Description.
  """
end
```

`vm_format` (see [syntax basics](./syntax_basics.livemd)) has a directive for exactly this two-step dance. `~o` resolves `print_object` on its argument automatically, then formats the result, so the cell above is the same as writing this in one step:

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  vm_format "~o~%" [switch_instance_1].
  """
end
```

Methods are printable objects too. `behaviour`, the class every class, category and method eventually falls under, overrides `print_object` to return a method's own source text instead of a class name. `listing/2` uses that to print every clause of a method: give it a class and a method name, and it finds the method and runs `print_object` on each of its clauses.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  listing switch flick.
  """
end
```

## Ephemeral Objects (Values)

So far we have only discussed 'durable' objects, without defining what this means. Each durable object in AL has a backing existence in the underlying Mnesia relation tables (which as you will see later, is just a RAM-based view over the command log).

Ephemeral/Value objects, on the other hand, only exist for the lifespan of a transaction, which means they do not clog up the database and are perfect for calculation. This means new objects can be generated 'on the fly' without dirtying the database.

A nice thing about ephemerals is that `new` gets to decide what the representation of an ephemeral actually is. In fact, numbers and lists are both ephemerals.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  super number value.
  """
end
```

We'll discuss the most interesting facets of ephemerals in [logic and constraints](./logic_and_constraints.livemd).

## Advanced: raw method dispatch

Everything so far has gone through ordinary dispatch: a send looks up which method answers a selector on a class, following its supers if it has to. `vm_oapply` skips inheritance chaining. You give it a method's own internal id directly, found with `method`, and it runs that exact method body, bypassing class/selector lookup entirely.

```elixir
run do
  ~AL"""
  method number factorial Id.
  vm_oapply Id [5, Result].
  """
end
```

This is how AL implements things like `call_next_method` under the hood. It is not advised to use this in ordinary code, although it may provide performance boosts since it does not require checking the inheritance chain.

## Some philosophy on 'Relational Objects'

As you may have noticed, relations about objects don't require the object to 'exist' in some sense. In fact, to say that an object exists in AL is to say that there are relations which concern that object: The ID is meaningless in itself, and is just a handle or pointer to establish sameness- the essence of the object lies in its relations.
